Logical: KGDak
Title: "Knowledge graph L2: Digital Adaptation Kit"
Description: "The kit itself. Properties are exactly the metadata fields DAK.fsh declares and dak.json carries -- no more, so that a dak node can be produced from a real file without anything being invented. canonicalUrl equals publicationUrl on a release branch and previewUrl on a development branch, per DAK.fsh. Both are kept because which one canonicalUrl currently equals is what says whether a graph describes a release or a build. Class IRI: http://smart.who.int/kg/dak."

* ^status = #active
* ^publisher = "World Health Organization (WHO)"
* id 0..1 string "Id" "The identifier the source declares (an FSH instance id, a BPMN or DMN element id)."
* name 0..1 string "Name" "The computable name: the CamelCase identifier a Canonical() reference matches on."
* title 0..1 string "Title" "The human-readable title."
* description 0..1 markdown "Description" "A description, as the source gives it."
* version 0..1 string "Version" "The business version, as declared."
* status 0..1 code "Status" "The DAK's publication status (DAK.fsh, FHIR PublicationStatus)."
* status from KGPublicationStatusVS (required)
* publicationUrl 0..1 uri "Publication URL" "Where the DAK is published (DAK.fsh)."
* previewUrl 0..1 uri "Preview URL" "Where the development build is previewed (DAK.fsh)."
* canonicalUrl 0..1 uri "Canonical URL" "The canonical base; equals publicationUrl on a release branch and previewUrl on a development branch."
* license 0..1 string "License" "The licence, as DAK.fsh declares it (an SPDX code)."
* copyrightYear 0..1 string "Copyright year" "The copyright year or range, as declared."
* publisherName 0..1 string "Publisher name" "The publisher's name."
* publisherUrl 0..1 uri "Publisher URL" "The publisher's web address."

Logical: KGPersona
Title: "Knowledge graph L2: Generic Persona"
Description: "DAK component 2. Grounded in GenericPersona.fsh -- title, id, description, otherNames, iscoCode bound to ISCO08ValueSet -- and in the 22 committed ActorDefinition instances that carry name, title, type and description. The two disagree about where ISCO lives, which the properties record rather than smooth over. `title` is the human-readable label a BPMN pool matches on; `name` is the CamelCase identifier a Canonical() reference matches on. They are different strings for the same persona and conflating them is precisely how the pool join breaks. GenericPersona.fsh declares `iscoCode 0..* code`, but the committed ActorDefinitions record ISCO in prose inside description, so iscoCode is usually empty and is not authoritative. Class IRI: http://smart.who.int/kg/persona."

* ^status = #active
* ^publisher = "World Health Organization (WHO)"
* id 0..1 string "Id" "The identifier the source declares (an FSH instance id, a BPMN or DMN element id)."
